# AniKo API — manual request collection
#
# Two hosts, because the interesting failures are the ones that only happen on one of them:
# CORS is a no-op locally and load-bearing in production, and the cold start below does not exist
# on a developer's machine at all. Switch by commenting out the other @host line.
#
# The Render hostname really is `aniko-api-4emi` — Render appends a suffix when the service name
# is taken. The bare `aniko-api.onrender.com` resolves and answers **404 from Render's edge**,
# not NXDOMAIN, so a typo here looks like a routing bug in this API rather than a wrong host.

@host = http://localhost:5199
# @host = https://aniko-api-4emi.onrender.com

# The free Render instance sleeps after ~15 minutes idle. A cold request takes ~22s wall clock
# (measured: 22.4s cold, 0.096s warm) and 36ms of that is the TCP connect — so a slow response
# here is the container booting, not the network, and not this API being slow. Send /health first
# to absorb the wake-up before timing anything else.

### Health — also the wake-up call for a sleeping instance
GET {{host}}/health
Accept: text/plain

### Service identity — which build is this, and what is it talking to?
GET {{host}}/
Accept: application/json

### OpenAPI document
GET {{host}}/openapi/v1.json
Accept: application/json


############################################################
# Dashboard — the five panels
############################################################

### Overview stats — the four tiles. No parameters; windows come from the server clock.
GET {{host}}/api/v1/buyer/overview/stats
Accept: application/json

### Price trends — default window (6 months, from the record's own default)
GET {{host}}/api/v1/pricing/trends
Accept: application/json

### Price trends — explicit window
GET {{host}}/api/v1/pricing/trends?months=12
Accept: application/json

### Nearby suppliers — origin is Manila. lat/lng are required, deliberately not defaulted.
GET {{host}}/api/v1/suppliers/nearby?lat=14.5995&lng=120.9842&limit=5
Accept: application/json

### Featured lots — default limit (10)
GET {{host}}/api/v1/listings/featured
Accept: application/json

### Recent orders — note `status` comes back lowercase and `id` is the human reference "AK-1003"
GET {{host}}/api/v1/orders/recent?limit=5
Accept: application/json


############################################################
# Rejections — these are the responses worth eyeballing
############################################################
# Every request below MUST fail, and the point of keeping them here is that the *shape* of the
# failure is part of the contract. A 200 from any of these is a bug, not a lenient API.

### months above the ceiling — 400 naming the parameter, NOT a silent clamp to 24
# A clamp would render a two-year chart for a caller who asked for eighty, produce no error
# anywhere, and leave the frontend bug undiscoverable.
GET {{host}}/api/v1/pricing/trends?months=999
Accept: application/json

### months below the floor — 400
GET {{host}}/api/v1/pricing/trends?months=0
Accept: application/json

### limit above the ceiling — 400
GET {{host}}/api/v1/orders/recent?limit=51
Accept: application/json

### Missing origin — 400, and `detail` names the parameter.
# This one is a regression test: the global handler used to answer 500 for every exception,
# including model-binding failures. The caller was told the server was broken and to retry,
# when retrying could never help, and it was logged at Error so a scan of the query string
# would bury real faults.
GET {{host}}/api/v1/suppliers/nearby
Accept: application/json

### Latitude off the globe — 400. Note that (0,0) is NOT rejected: it is a real coordinate in
# the Gulf of Guinea, which is exactly why lat/lng have no default.
GET {{host}}/api/v1/suppliers/nearby?lat=999&lng=0
Accept: application/json

### Non-numeric coordinates — 400 from model binding, not 500
GET {{host}}/api/v1/suppliers/nearby?lat=abc&lng=def
Accept: application/json


############################################################
# CORS — only meaningful against the deployed host
############################################################
# CORS failures are invisible from here. The server answers 200 with clean logs and curl works
# perfectly; only a browser refuses, and only because the response lacks a header. So the thing
# to inspect below is the *headers*, not the status or the body.

### Preflight from the real frontend origin — expect Access-Control-Allow-Origin echoed back
OPTIONS {{host}}/api/v1/orders/recent
Origin: https://aniko-agri.netlify.app
Access-Control-Request-Method: GET

### Preflight from an origin that is not allowed — expect NO Access-Control-Allow-Origin header.
# The status will still be 2xx/4xx and the body will still look fine. Absence of the header is
# the entire signal.
OPTIONS {{host}}/api/v1/orders/recent
Origin: https://not-aniko.example.com
Access-Control-Request-Method: GET

###
